article_page('effect-of-price-hike-in-sale-agreement', <<<'HTML'
<p>A sale agreement is a binding contract that fixes the terms of a property transaction, including the price. Disputes often arise when the market value rises sharply between signing the agreement and executing the sale deed, and the seller tries to back out or demand more money. Can a price increase justify changing the deal?</p>
<h2>The sanctity of contracts</h2>
<p>Contracts are built on the principle that parties are bound by the terms they freely agreed to. <strong>Specific performance</strong> is the remedy that allows one party to ask the court to enforce the contract as written, compelling the other side to complete the sale.</p>
<h2>When property prices rise</h2>
<p>When a property's value increases significantly after the agreement, sellers sometimes refuse to execute the sale deed, claiming hardship because the agreed price is now too low.</p>
<p>Courts have consistently held that market changes, or a price that now looks inadequate, are <strong>not grounds to deny specific performance</strong>. Price movements are a normal commercial risk that both parties accept when they enter into a contract.</p>
<h2>What the Supreme Court has said</h2>
<p>The Supreme Court has made clear that a rise in property prices after the date of the contract does not, by itself, amount to "hardship" that would justify refusing specific performance. The terms are fixed when the agreement is signed.</p>
<p>In exceptional cases, courts have directed the buyer to pay an amount above the agreed price, for example where the buyer's own delay contributed to the situation. These are exceptions; the agreed price generally stands.</p>
<h2>What this means for you</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Buyers:</strong> keep proof that you were always ready and willing to pay the balance, and act promptly if the seller backs out. Delay on your part can weaken a claim for specific performance.</li>
<li><strong>Sellers:</strong> a rise in value is not a legal reason to cancel. Get advice before refusing to perform.</li>
<li><strong>Both:</strong> draft the sale agreement carefully, with clear timelines, payment terms and consequences of default.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>Contracts are the foundation of fair and predictable property transactions. Upholding agreements as written, even when prices move, lets buyers and sellers deal with confidence and keeps commercial and real estate transactions stable.</p>
